From 518b5e345307f571c2f8633b8ee642edabab99dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: spiritedman4 <sivamsrini214@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:29:12 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Fix duplicate Drive folders and the races behind them

A first sync left three or four copies of every folder on Drive, with the
vault's files scattered across them. There were four distinct defects, each
independently sufficient to cause it.

Folder resolution raced within a sync. ensureParentFolder dropped its lock
across the network call, so N workers pushing into the same missing
directory all saw an empty list and all created it. New folderCache resolves
each directory exactly once via per-path single-flight, publishing the entry
before the call. Entries are per path component, so siblings still resolve
concurrently while sharing one resolution of their ancestor.

Folder resolution raced across syncs. Drive's files.list is backed by an
eventually consistent search index, so a folder created moments earlier can
be missing from the next sync's lookup and created again. Manifest now
persists a dir -> folder ID map (schema v2, migrated from v1 by starting
empty). A remembered ID is verified by files.get, which is strongly
consistent, before reuse; stale mappings (trashed, moved, gone) are dropped
and recreated. A failed verification fails the operation rather than
guessing "absent" -- guessing is what creates duplicates.

Syncs could overlap. A manual sync and a vault-event batch ran together,
each with its own folderCache, so each created its own copy of every
directory and every file was uploaded twice. Sync RPCs now serialize on a
capacity-1 semaphore held across result streaming, blocking rather than
rejecting so a queued batch is delayed instead of dropped.

Plugin startup enqueued the whole vault. Obsidian replays a create event for
every existing file while building its initial index, and watchers were
registered in onload, turning startup into a full sync that then ran
alongside any real one. Watchers now register inside onLayoutReady.

Also fixes two hazards found while tracing the above:

Repointing the Drive root silently stranded the baseline. Records describe
an agreement with one specific folder tree; against a different root they
describe files that have nothing to do with it, so pushes kept updating the
old folder's files in place while a full sync read the new folder's
emptiness as a wholesale remote deletion. The manifest now records its root
and discards the baseline when it changes. An unrecorded root is adopted
rather than treated as a change, so existing vaults are not wiped on
upgrade.

Changing the root folder name did nothing. SetConfig saved the name but kept
the cached ID, and initDrive only resolves a name when the ID is empty, so
the daemon kept syncing into the old folder. It now clears the ID on a real
name change and re-resolves.

Adds a guard refusing a full sync whose Drive listing is empty while the
baseline says files should exist -- an empty listing means the root was
repointed, trashed, or access was revoked far more often than it means the
user deleted everything, and acting on it deletes the local vault.

EnsureSubfolders is removed: unused after this, and unsafe by construction.

Known gaps, unaddressed here: push still trusts baseline.DriveFileID without
checking the file is live and in the expected parent, so UpdateContent can
write into a trashed or duplicate folder and report success; and there is no
guard for the mirror case of an empty local scan against a full baseline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
